            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for out of one's mind, then the Latin for to be delirious."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: crūdēlitās, crūdēlitātis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "crūdēlitās, crūdēlitātis — \"cruelty\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Crūdēlitās dominī servōs terret. The master's cruelty frightens the slaves."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, third declension, from crūdēlis. English cruelty."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "crūdēlitās is built on crūdēlis \"cruel\", itself related to crūdus \"raw\" (Lewis & Short). English cruelty comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
